CAPTURE INSTRUMENT, no graded answer. It stages six mutually hostile players on one map,
photographs the minimap panel once, and asserts nothing about the colours in it. A human
reads the PNG against the pixel table in description.txt and decides whether adjacent
enemy reds are separable at 2.3 px.

WHY SKIP AND NOT PASS. The Lua evaluates no predicate about the mod. Its two runtime
checks -- that every owner has its expected marker count, and that MapLayers.GetVisibility
reads 10 at fifteen sampled cells so the minimap's shroud overlay is transparent -- are
guards on the INSTRUMENT, not measurements of the subject. They can only downgrade the
frame to "not evidence"; they can never conclude anything about shade legibility. A
Test.Pass would contribute a green to every run-batch.sh --all tally whether or not the
capture landed, which is the exact mislabelling corrected across this suite on 2026-09-01.

The shade arithmetic itself is already graded, and elsewhere: RelationshipShadeTest.cs
pins the step schedule, the light-to-dark ordering, the no-collision property and hue
preservation. Nothing here should duplicate that.

REMOVE THIS FILE in the same commit as whatever gives the scenario a real assertion. The
obvious candidate is a Lua binding that returns a player's resolved minimap colour, which
would let the run assert that six distinct colours actually reached AppearsOnMiniMap
rather than inferring it from a rainbow-vs-ramp tell in the image. That binding does not
exist today and was deliberately not added blind; see the branch's Watch notes.

A PASS against a live `skip` declaration grades RED on purpose, so a scenario that starts
grading announces itself instead of silently changing meaning.

NOT EXPOSED TO expected-status.sh's KNOWN GAP. That gap is specific to `fail`
declarations, where a watchdog TIMEOUT-FAIL also exits 1 and therefore grades green.
A timeout reaches expected_status_grade as FAIL, and declared-`skip` + FAIL is RED.
